MCQ = [
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Cognitive Processes","page":304,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1 points readers of the eight-step problem-solving method to which chapter for Osborn-Parnes thinking techniques?","correct":"Chapter 16, Developing Ideas","w1":"Chapter 24, Military Customs and Courtesies","w2":"Chapter 22, Fitness and Readiness","w3":"Chapter 11, Personnel Programs","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 says Osborn-Parnes convergent and divergent techniques live in the Developing Ideas chapter. That is Chapter 16, an E-6 catalog chapter."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Cognitive Processes","page":304,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"When AFH 1 tells teams to defer judgment while brainstorming countermeasures, the point is what?","correct":"Let ideas surface before they are graded, then select","w1":"Accept the first idea because speed is the root cause","w2":"Insult weak ideas so people stop talking","w3":"Skip Step 4 and standardize immediately","explain":"Paragraph 13.13 says to defer judgment and let ideas flow, including odd ones, before evaluation. That technique is tied to the Developing Ideas chapter."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Divergent Thinking","page":306,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Divergent thinking, as AFH 1 uses it beside problem solving, is best described as what?","correct":"Opening the field of options before narrowing","w1":"Choosing the first familiar fix","w2":"Copying last year's slide","w3":"Stopping the discussion to protect a pet idea","explain":"The handbook contrasts divergent idea-finding with later convergent selection. Grabbing the familiar fix too early skips the divergent pass."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Convergent Thinking","page":306,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Convergent thinking in the Developing Ideas discussion does what?","correct":"Narrows options against the actual goal and the data","w1":"Adds unrelated ideas forever","w2":"Ignores the problem statement","w3":"Replaces the need for a decision","explain":"Convergent thinking selects. Divergent thinking expands. A decision still has to be made against the goal, not against whoever spoke last."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16C","para":"16C","title":"Informed Decision-Making","page":313,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"An informed decision, in AFH 1's Developing Ideas chapter, depends on what?","correct":"Relevant information, not rank alone and not rumor","w1":"The loudest voice in the room","w2":"A decision made before the data exists","w3":"Hiding uncertainty from everyone","explain":"Section 16C is about informed decision-making. Volume and rank do not replace the information the decision needs."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16A","para":"16A","title":"What We Know","page":304,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"AFH 1's section What We Know asks Airmen to separate what?","correct":"Supported facts from assumptions they have not checked","w1":"Friends from the rest of the flight","w2":"Pay grades from duty titles only","w3":"Leave types from travel vouchers","explain":"Section 16A is about the knowledge you actually have. Treating an assumption as a fact is how bad decisions get confident."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16D","para":"16D","title":"What We Don't Know","page":318,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"The point of AFH 1's What We Don't Know section is what?","correct":"Name the gaps before you pretend the plan is complete","w1":"Hide gaps so the brief looks clean","w2":"Stop seeking information once a guess feels good","w3":"Treat every unknown as someone else's job with no owner","explain":"Section 16D pushes Airmen to see the boundary of their knowledge. A clean brief that hides the gap is not informed."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16C","para":"16C","title":"Analytical Thinking","page":313,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"Analytical thinking, as a foundational competency used in Developing Ideas, shows up as what behavior?","correct":"Breaking a problem into parts and testing how those parts connect","w1":"Defending the first story because changing it looks weak","w2":"Collecting opinions and calling them data","w3":"Skipping the cause and writing the fix","explain":"Analytical thinking is the discipline of taking the problem apart. Jumping to a fix before the parts are clear is the miss the problem-solving chapter warns about."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Creative Thinking","page":306,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Creative thinking in this chapter is useful when the team does what?","correct":"Generates options that are not just repeats of the last fix","w1":"Refuses any idea that is unfamiliar","w2":"Standardizes a broken process to save time","w3":"Lets one person own every idea","explain":"Creative thinking widens the option set. Standardization comes after a countermeasure works, not before the team has options."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16C","para":"16C","title":"Influence","page":313,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"Influence, among the idea competencies, is the ability to do what?","correct":"Move people toward a sound course of action without pretending the facts are optional","w1":"Order compliance and skip the reason","w2":"Trade favors that hide a safety miss","w3":"Win the argument by volume","explain":"Influence is how an idea becomes action. It still has to be tied to the information, not to pressure that conceals a hazard."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16B","para":"16B","title":"Fostering Innovation","page":306,"diff":3,"ranks":"e6","question":"Fostering innovation fails when a leader does what?","correct":"Punishes the person who surfaced a flaw in the current process","w1":"Asks what the data shows","w2":"Gives the idea a small trial","w3":"Connects the idea to the mission","explain":"Innovation needs a safe path from idea to test. Punishing the messenger teaches the flight to protect the broken process."},
{"ch":16,"sec":"16D","para":"16D","title":"Information Seeking","page":318,"diff":2,"ranks":"e6","question":"Information seeking, applied to Developing Ideas, means what?","correct":"Going to get the missing fact instead of decorating the guess","w1":"Waiting until someone else is curious","w2":"Asking only people who already agree","w3":"Stopping at the first search result that feels familiar","explain":"Information seeking is active. A familiar result that does not answer the question is not the end of the search."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17F","para":"17F","title":"Risk Management","page":336,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1's risk-management cycle in section 17F follows which order?","correct":"Identify hazards, assess hazards, develop controls and decide, implement controls, supervise and evaluate","w1":"Supervise first, then look for hazards if someone is hurt","w2":"Implement a control, then decide whether a hazard exists","w3":"Skip assessment when the mission is familiar","explain":"Section 17F teaches the five-step risk-management cycle. Familiarity does not delete the assessment or the supervision step."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17F","para":"17F","title":"Risk Management","page":336,"diff":2,"question":"After controls are in place, AFH 1's risk-management model still requires what?","correct":"Supervision and evaluation to see if the control works","w1":"Assuming the hazard is gone forever","w2":"A new hazard hunt only after a Class A mishap","w3":"Deleting the risk from the brief","explain":"The last step is supervise and evaluate. A control that is not watched is a hope, not a control."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17A","para":"17A","title":"Emergency Management","page":321,"diff":2,"question":"The Air Force emergency-management program in Chapter 17 is aimed at what?","correct":"Preparing the installation and its people for attacks, disasters, and hazards","w1":"Replacing the flying schedule","w2":"Writing promotion tests","w3":"Setting clothing allowances","explain":"Section 17A frames emergency management as preparedness for the hazards that can hit an installation. It is not a pay or testing program."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17B","para":"17B","title":"Hazards","page":323,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 groups the threats in the emergency-management chapter to include what?","correct":"Natural disasters, major accidents, and hostile attacks, including CBRN hazards","w1":"Only weather delays for leave travel","w2":"Only uniform discrepancies","w3":"Only peacetime sports injuries","explain":"Section 17B covers disasters, attacks, and hazards, including chemical, biological, radiological, and nuclear threats. A late leave return is not the chapter's subject."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17C","para":"17C","title":"Preparedness","page":325,"diff":2,"question":"Preparedness in AFH 1 Chapter 17 means the unit does what before the incident?","correct":"Plans, trains, and equips so the response is not invented during the crisis","w1":"Waits for the event and then writes the plan","w2":"Assigns the plan to one person and does not train","w3":"Assumes home station cannot be hit","explain":"Section 17C is preparedness and protection. A plan that has never been trained is not preparedness."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17D","para":"17D","title":"Response and Recovery","page":330,"diff":2,"question":"Crisis response and recovery in AFH 1 are different because recovery does what?","correct":"Restores the mission and the community after the immediate danger is contained","w1":"Is the same minute as the initial alarm","w2":"Ends when the first announcement is made","w3":"Is optional if the response looked busy","explain":"Section 17D separates the immediate response from the longer work of recovery. Declaring the emergency is not the end of the job."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17E","para":"17E","title":"Mishap Prevention","page":332,"diff":2,"question":"Mishap prevention in AFH 1 belongs with emergency management because both ask Airmen to do what?","correct":"Remove or control hazards before people and equipment are hurt","w1":"Report only the mishaps that make the unit look careful","w2":"Wait for a safety day once a year","w3":"Treat near misses as good news that needs no report","explain":"Section 17E ties mishap prevention to finding hazards early. A near miss is information, not a trophy."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17F","para":"17F","title":"Risk Decisions","page":336,"diff":3,"question":"In the risk-management steps, developing controls comes before what?","correct":"The decision to accept or reject the remaining risk","w1":"Identifying that a hazard exists","w2":"Any look at who could be hurt","w3":"The mission existing","explain":"You cannot make a serious risk decision until hazards are identified, assessed, and matched with controls. Acceptance without that work is a guess."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17C","para":"17C","title":"Protection","page":325,"diff":3,"question":"Protection measures in the preparedness section are useful only if people do what?","correct":"Know the measure and can execute it under stress","w1":"File the measure and never brief it","w2":"Assume the shelter is obvious","w3":"Wait for a perfect warning","explain":"A protection measure that lives only in a binder fails the preparedness test. People have to be able to do it when the warning is short."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17A","para":"17A","title":"All-Hazards","page":321,"diff":2,"question":"An all-hazards approach in AFH 1 emergency management means what?","correct":"One preparedness system covers natural, technological, and adversary hazards","w1":"Only enemy attack is in scope","w2":"Only weather is in scope","w3":"Each shop invents a private doctrine","explain":"The program is built so the installation is not surprised by the category of hazard. Private doctrines fracture the response."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17D","para":"17D","title":"Recovery","page":330,"diff":3,"question":"A recovery goal after a crisis, as Chapter 17 frames it, includes what?","correct":"Returning essential mission capability, not only cleaning up the visible damage","w1":"Holding a pizza party and calling the event closed","w2":"Erasing the after-action notes","w3":"Skipping medical follow-up","explain":"Recovery is about capability and people, not only the appearance of the site. After-action notes are how the next event gets cheaper."},
{"ch":17,"sec":"17E","para":"17E","title":"Mishap Prevention","page":332,"diff":3,"question":"Who owns mishap prevention on a shift, according to the spirit of AFH 1's safety section?","correct":"Every Airman who can see the hazard, with supervisors accountable for the system","w1":"Only the safety office after an injury","w2":"Only the newest Airman","w3":"Nobody, if the mission is important","explain":"Section 17E does not park prevention in a single office. Supervisors own the system, and the person who sees the hazard owns the warning."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18B","para":"18B","title":"Operations Security","page":346,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1's OPSEC process uses which sequence?","correct":"Identify critical information, analyze threats, analyze vulnerabilities, assess risk, apply countermeasures","w1":"Publish the critical information so adversaries stop guessing","w2":"Buy equipment first and define the secret later","w3":"Assess risk only after a leak is in the news","explain":"Section 18B teaches the five OPSEC steps in that order. Countermeasures come after you know what is critical and how it can leak."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18B","para":"18B","title":"Critical Information","page":346,"diff":2,"question":"Critical information in OPSEC is best defined as what?","correct":"Facts an adversary could use to act against the mission","w1":"Any fact that would embarrass a supervisor","w2":"Only information marked Top Secret","w3":"The unit's morale-shirt design","explain":"OPSEC protects information that is useful to an adversary, even when it is unclassified. Embarrassment and fashion are not the test."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18C","para":"18C","title":"Information Protection","page":348,"diff":2,"question":"Which set does AFH 1 treat as the classified information levels?","correct":"Top Secret, Secret, and Confidential","w1":"FOUO, Personal, and Draft","w2":"Sensitive, Internal, and Public","w3":"Red, amber, and green","explain":"Section 18C covers classification. Controlled unclassified information is a separate category and is not itself a classification level."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18C","para":"18C","title":"Need to Know","page":348,"diff":2,"question":"A security clearance alone, in the information-protection rules AFH 1 teaches, is not enough because access also requires what?","correct":"A need to know for that information","w1":"Seniority in the squadron","w2":"A friendship with the briefer","w3":"Curiosity","explain":"Section 18C pairs eligibility with need to know. Rank and curiosity do not authorize access."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18E","para":"18E","title":"FPCON","page":357,"diff":1,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1's antiterrorism section uses which force-protection condition scale?","correct":"Normal, Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, and Delta","w1":"Green, blue, yellow, orange, and red as the Air Force FPCON names","w2":"Defcon 1 through Defcon 5 as the installation scale","w3":"Low, medium, and high only","explain":"Section 18E uses FPCON Normal, Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, and Delta. Defense readiness conditions are a different system."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18E","para":"18E","title":"FPCON Delta","page":357,"diff":3,"question":"FPCON Delta, in the scale AFH 1 teaches, means what relative to the other conditions?","correct":"The highest condition, applied when a terrorist attack has occurred or is imminent","w1":"The everyday peacetime baseline","w2":"A drill that never changes access","w3":"A state with no extra measures","explain":"Delta is the top of the FPCON scale and is used for an imminent or actual attack. Normal is the baseline, not Delta."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18A","para":"18A","title":"Integrated Defense","page":342,"diff":2,"question":"Integrated defense in AFH 1 means the installation protects itself by doing what?","correct":"Combining security disciplines instead of letting each one operate blind","w1":"Relying on a single gate guard with no plan","w2":"Publishing patrol routes for convenience","w3":"Ignoring insider threats","explain":"Section 18A describes integrated defense as a combined approach. A single visible post is not the whole defense."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18D","para":"18D","title":"Cyber Security","page":353,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 puts cyber security with information access because a network account is what?","correct":"A key to government information, not a personal convenience","w1":"Something safe to share with a spouse","w2":"Fine to use on any public kiosk for official mail","w3":"Impossible to misuse if the user means well","explain":"Section 18D treats access and mobility as security issues. Sharing credentials or bypassing controls is a security event, not a shortcut."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18C","para":"18C","title":"CUI","page":348,"diff":3,"question":"Controlled unclassified information in AFH 1's protection section is what?","correct":"Unclassified information that still requires safeguarding and is not the same as Secret","w1":"A fourth classification level above Top Secret","w2":"Information with no handling rules","w3":"Automatically releasable to anyone with a CAC","explain":"CUI is not a classification. It still has handling rules, and a CAC alone is not a release authority."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18B","para":"18B","title":"OPSEC Indicators","page":346,"diff":3,"question":"An OPSEC indicator is dangerous because it does what?","correct":"Lets an adversary piece together critical information from small clues","w1":"Replaces the need for a classification guide","w2":"Is harmless if each clue is unclassified","w3":"Only counts if it is posted by a commander","explain":"OPSEC exists because unclassified pieces can combine into a useful picture. The poster's rank does not make the clue safe."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18E","para":"18E","title":"Antiterrorism","page":357,"diff":2,"question":"Antiterrorism measures in AFH 1 are meant to do what?","correct":"Reduce vulnerability to attack and raise the cost for an attacker","w1":"Guarantee that no attack can ever occur","w2":"Replace intelligence","w3":"Apply only to deployed locations","explain":"Section 18E is about reducing vulnerability at home and deployed. No measure promises a world with zero threat."},
{"ch":18,"sec":"18A","para":"18A","title":"Integrated Defense","page":342,"diff":3,"question":"A random antiterrorism measure works better than a published pattern because it does what?","correct":"Denies the adversary a reliable picture of the defense","w1":"Confuses the defenders on purpose with no plan","w2":"Replaces training","w3":"Is required to be announced the day before","explain":"Predictable patterns are vulnerabilities. Randomness still has to sit on top of a trained plan or it is just noise."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19B","para":"19B","title":"Law of War","page":365,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"Which principles does AFH 1 expect Airmen to associate with the law of war?","correct":"Military necessity, humanity, distinction, proportionality, and honor","w1":"Speed, surprise, mass, and morale only","w2":"Rank, time in service, and decorations","w3":"Budget, manpower, and the POM","explain":"Section 19B teaches the law-of-war principles: necessity, humanity, distinction, proportionality, and honor. Principles of war are a different list in the doctrine chapter."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19B","para":"19B","title":"Distinction","page":365,"diff":3,"question":"The law-of-war principle of distinction requires forces to do what?","correct":"Separate military objectives from civilians and civilian objects","w1":"Treat every person in the area as a combatant","w2":"Ignore markings on protected sites","w3":"Strike the largest building because it is easiest","explain":"Distinction is the duty to tell combatants and military objectives apart from protected people and places. Convenience is not a principle."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19B","para":"19B","title":"Proportionality","page":365,"diff":3,"question":"Proportionality, in the law-of-war sense AFH 1 covers, limits what?","correct":"Attacks expected to cause civilian harm excessive to the concrete military advantage","w1":"The number of people on a promotion board","w2":"How much leave an Airman may take","w3":"The length of a staff package","explain":"Proportionality is about the expected civilian harm measured against a concrete military advantage. It is not a personnel-management ratio."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19C","para":"19C","title":"Code of Conduct","page":371,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"Article V of the Code of Conduct, as AFH 1 teaches it, requires a prisoner to give which information?","correct":"Name, rank, service number, and date of birth","w1":"Name only, and nothing else even if lawful","w2":"The unit's entire mission plan","w3":"The names of everyone in the unit","explain":"Section 19C includes Article V: when questioned, a prisoner gives name, rank, service number, and date of birth, and evades answering further."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19C","para":"19C","title":"Code of Conduct","page":371,"diff":2,"question":"How many articles does the Code of Conduct contain in the AFH 1 standards chapter?","correct":"Six","w1":"Three","w2":"Ten","w3":"Twelve","explain":"Section 19C presents the six articles of the Code of Conduct, from the opening identity article through the duty to remain responsible for one's actions."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19C","para":"19C","title":"Code of Conduct Article II","page":371,"diff":3,"question":"Article II of the Code of Conduct says a member will surrender only when?","correct":"They are no longer able to fight, and never of their own free will","w1":"The mission becomes inconvenient","w2":"A rumor says the unit is surrounded","w3":"They have been in grade for a year","explain":"Article II forbids surrender of one's own free will. Inconvenience and rumor are not the standard."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19A","para":"19A","title":"Way of Life","page":362,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1's standards-of-conduct chapter treats on-duty and off-duty behavior as what?","correct":"Both part of the professional standard, because the public trusts the uniform","w1":"Unrelated, so off-duty conduct cannot matter","w2":"Optional once the workday ends","w3":"Enforced only for officers","explain":"Section 19A describes the Air Force way of life as a continuing standard. The oath does not clock out, and it is not officer-only."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19B","para":"19B","title":"Humanity","page":365,"diff":3,"question":"The humanity principle in the law of war forbids what?","correct":"Weapons and methods calculated to cause unnecessary suffering","w1":"Any use of force","w2":"Distinguishing civilians from combatants","w3":"Reporting a violation","explain":"Humanity, sometimes discussed as unnecessary suffering, limits how force is applied. It does not ban lawful force or the duty to distinguish."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19C","para":"19C","title":"Code of Conduct Article IV","page":371,"diff":3,"question":"Article IV of the Code of Conduct tells a prisoner to keep faith with what?","correct":"Fellow prisoners","w1":"The captor's propaganda","w2":"Only their own comfort","w3":"A promise to accept special favors","explain":"Article IV is about keeping faith with other prisoners and refusing special favors that hurt them. Accepting a deal that isolates you from them breaks that article."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19B","para":"19B","title":"Honor","page":365,"diff":2,"question":"The law-of-war principle of honor, as taught for Airmen, demands what?","correct":"A sense of fairness and a refusal to use perfidy or bad-faith tricks","w1":"Winning by any deception, including abusing a protected symbol","w2":"Silence about violations by friendly forces","w3":"Matching the enemy's unlawful methods","explain":"Honor in this chapter is not a blank check for trickery. Perfidy and copying an unlawful method both fail it."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19A","para":"19A","title":"Way of Life","page":362,"diff":2,"question":"AFI 1-1 shows up in the standards chapter because it does what?","correct":"Sets the baseline personal standards for Airmen","w1":"Publishes aircraft technical orders","w2":"Is the joint publication for operational design","w3":"Is the travel-card regulation","explain":"AFI 1-1 is the standards instruction the handbook keeps pointing back to. Technical orders and the Joint Travel Regulations are different books."},
{"ch":19,"sec":"19C","para":"19C","title":"Code of Conduct Article VI","page":371,"diff":2,"question":"Article VI closes the Code of Conduct by reminding a member that they what?","correct":"Remain responsible for their actions and dedicated to the principles that made them free","w1":"May abandon the other articles once captured","w2":"Owe loyalty to the captor after 30 days","w3":"Should sign any statement that reduces questioning","explain":"Article VI keeps responsibility in the member's hands. Capture does not retire the other articles."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20A","para":"20A","title":"Inspection System","page":377,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1's Air Force Inspection System exists to do what?","correct":"Assess readiness, compliance, and continuous improvement","w1":"Replace commander responsibility","w2":"Score only the appearance of the parking lot","w3":"Write the promotion test","explain":"Section 20A and the cross-reference in Chapter 13 tie the inspection system, including AFI 90-201, to readiness, compliance, and improvement. Commanders still own the mission."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20B","para":"20B","title":"Individual Accountability","page":380,"diff":2,"question":"Individual accountability in Chapter 20 means a member answers for what?","correct":"Their own conduct and the duties they were trusted with","w1":"Only the mistakes of the previous shift","w2":"Nothing, if no one wrote it down the same day","w3":"Only events a commander personally witnessed","explain":"Section 20B places accountability on the member. Lack of a same-day memo does not erase the duty."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20C","para":"20C","title":"Working Relationships","page":385,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1 treats an unprofessional relationship as one that does what?","correct":"Compromises, or reasonably appears to compromise, the chain of command or good order","w1":"Is any friendship between coworkers","w2":"Is automatically fine if both people consent","w3":"Matters only if it is posted online","explain":"Section 20C focuses on relationships that break, or appear to break, impartiality, the chain, or good order. Ordinary professional friendship is not the target."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20C","para":"20C","title":"Favoritism","page":385,"diff":3,"question":"Favoritism becomes a standards problem in AFH 1 when a relationship causes what?","correct":"Preferential treatment or misuse of position","w1":"Two Airmen to eat lunch","w2":"A supervisor to give everyone the same feedback","w3":"A clear, job-based tasking","explain":"The harm is the use of position for preference. Shared meals and fair taskings are not the violation."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20D","para":"20D","title":"Addressing Misconduct","page":394,"diff":2,"question":"Progressive discipline, as AFH 1 frames it, is meant to do what?","correct":"Correct behavior at the lowest effective level and escalate if it continues","w1":"Start with the harshest punishment every time","w2":"Ignore misconduct until a board","w3":"Replace the commander with a peer vote","explain":"Section 20D describes addressing misconduct in a measured way that can escalate. Jumping to the maximum, or doing nothing, both miss the purpose."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20D","para":"20D","title":"Standards","page":394,"diff":2,"question":"A standard that is enforced for one Airman and ignored for another fails Chapter 20 because it does what?","correct":"Destroys the credibility of the standard itself","w1":"Shows flexibility, which is always the goal","w2":"Is required by the inspection system","w3":"Protects morale","explain":"Uneven enforcement teaches the flight that the rule is personal. Chapter 20's accountability sections exist to prevent that."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20A","para":"20A","title":"Commander's Inspection","page":377,"diff":3,"question":"The inspection system still leaves which duty with the commander?","correct":"Responsibility for the unit's compliance and readiness between inspections","w1":"No duty until an inspector arrives","w2":"The duty to hide defects until the visit ends","w3":"The duty to write the inspector's report for them","explain":"Inspections sample a unit. They do not suspend the commander's daily responsibility, and hiding defects is a standards failure of its own."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20C","para":"20C","title":"Trainee Relationships","page":385,"diff":3,"question":"Relationships across a training or supervisory line draw extra scrutiny in AFH 1 because they can do what?","correct":"Make consent look free when authority makes it not free","w1":"Always improve training outcomes","w2":"Be ignored if the grades are good","w3":"Replace the need for a feedback session","explain":"Section 20C is especially concerned where one person can affect the other's career or training. Good grades do not cure the power difference."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20B","para":"20B","title":"Reporting","page":380,"diff":3,"question":"Seeing a standards breach and saying nothing is treated in this chapter as what?","correct":"A choice that can make the witness part of the failure","w1":"The preferred way to protect the team","w2":"Required if the person is a friend","w3":"Neutral, because only the actor is involved","explain":"Accountability sections in Chapters 15 and 20 both reject the idea that silence is neutral when the mission or a person is at risk."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20D","para":"20D","title":"Administrative Tools","page":394,"diff":3,"question":"Administrative action for misconduct, as Chapter 20 discusses it, is different from a court-martial because it is what?","correct":"A command tool to correct or document behavior short of a criminal trial","w1":"Always a federal criminal conviction","w2":"The same thing as a decoration","w3":"Available only to general officers","explain":"Section 20D covers command options for misconduct. Criminal trials live in the military-justice chapter, which is not on the 2026 PFE chapter list."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20A","para":"20A","title":"Self-Assessment","page":377,"diff":2,"question":"A useful self-assessment under the inspection system does what?","correct":"Finds the defect while the unit can still fix it","w1":"Copies last year's green slide","w2":"Rates every program perfect to protect the boss","w3":"Is written by the inspector and signed unread","explain":"Self-assessment is only useful if it tells the truth early. A perfect slide that hides the gap is the defect."},
{"ch":20,"sec":"20C","para":"20C","title":"Good Order","page":385,"diff":2,"question":"Good order and discipline in AFH 1 is the condition where what is true?","correct":"People can trust that rules apply and the mission comes before private deals","w1":"Nobody ever disagrees","w2":"Rank replaces the need for competence","w3":"Friendship outranks the checklist","explain":"Good order is trust in fair rules and mission priority. It is not the absence of professional disagreement."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22A","para":"22A","title":"Fitness","page":414,"diff":2,"question":"AFH 1 ties the fitness program to which purpose?","correct":"A ready force that can do the mission, not a cosmetic contest","w1":"A way to rank roommates","w2":"A substitute for job qualification","w3":"An optional hobby for deployers only","explain":"Section 22A frames fitness as readiness. It is not a popularity score and it does not replace occupational qualification."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22B","para":"22B","title":"Fitness Assessment","page":420,"diff":2,"question":"The official fitness assessment in AFH 1 is best described as what?","correct":"A standardized check of readiness against the current Air Force instruction","w1":"A unit-invented workout with no standard","w2":"A one-time test at basic training only","w3":"A measure of how fast someone finishes a staff package","explain":"Section 22B points to the official assessment. The numeric components change by instruction, so the handbook's durable point is that the assessment is official and current."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22C","para":"22C","title":"Nutrition","page":425,"diff":2,"question":"Nutrition shows up beside fitness in AFH 1 because performance depends on what?","correct":"Fuel and recovery, not exercise alone","w1":"Skipping meals to make a weight goal at any cost","w2":"Supplements with no food","w3":"Energy drinks as a meal plan","explain":"Section 22C treats nutrition as part of readiness. Extreme shortcuts that harm the member are not the program."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22D","para":"22D","title":"Substance Abuse","page":429,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1's substance abuse section expects Airmen to treat illegal drug use as what?","correct":"Incompatible with service and with a ready force","w1":"A private choice with no duty impact","w2":"Acceptable off duty if the member is a good technician","w3":"Required for shift work","explain":"Section 22D places substance abuse against readiness and standards. Skill on the job does not license illegal use."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22D","para":"22D","title":"Alcohol","page":429,"diff":3,"question":"Responsible alcohol standards in this chapter exist because misuse does what?","correct":"Damages health, judgment, and the people who have to count on the member","w1":"Improves alertness for duty","w2":"Is invisible to the unit if it happens off base","w3":"Counts as a fitness credit","explain":"Section 22D connects alcohol misuse to health and to the team that depends on a clear-headed member. Off-base does not mean off-standard."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22E","para":"22E","title":"Readiness State of Mind","page":433,"diff":2,"question":"A readiness state of mind in AFH 1 means the member does what?","correct":"Stays mentally prepared to deploy and to do the job under stress","w1":"Assumes someone else will be ready","w2":"Treats mental preparation as unrelated to the mission","w3":"Waits for the deployment order to start preparing","explain":"Section 22E is the mental piece of readiness. Preparation that starts at the order is already late."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22A","para":"22A","title":"Wingman Fitness","page":414,"diff":2,"question":"Fitness culture in AFH 1 fails when a flight does what?","correct":"Mocks a struggling member instead of helping them meet the standard","w1":"Trains together","w2":"Knows the current instruction","w3":"Treats medical profiles as real","explain":"The standard stays. Ridicule does not raise readiness. Profiles and help are part of getting people to the standard safely."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22B","para":"22B","title":"Currency","page":420,"diff":3,"question":"Why should an Airman check the current fitness instruction instead of an old practice test?","correct":"Components and scoring change, and the current instruction controls","w1":"The handbook freezes the numbers forever","w2":"Old scores always transfer without a rule","w3":"Unit tradition outranks the instruction","explain":"AFH 1 points to the official program. Numeric details move faster than a party-game card, so the current instruction is the authority."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22E","para":"22E","title":"Help","page":433,"diff":2,"question":"Seeking help for a mental-readiness problem is treated in this section as what?","correct":"A way to stay in the fight, not a failure of toughness","w1":"An automatic end to a career with no care offered","w2":"Something only commanders may do","w3":"A substitute for hiding the problem","explain":"Readiness includes the willingness to get help early. Toughness that hides a breaking point costs the mission the person."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22C","para":"22C","title":"Nutrition","page":425,"diff":2,"question":"A deployment diet that ignores hydration and regular meals undercuts which AFH 1 point?","correct":"Nutrition is a readiness input, especially when the environment is hard","w1":"Calories do not matter downrange","w2":"Only the fitness assessment week matters","w3":"Supplements replace water","explain":"Section 22C connects food and hydration to performance. The assessment week is not the only week the body works."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22D","para":"22D","title":"Prescription Misuse","page":429,"diff":3,"question":"Misusing a prescription, in the substance section, is a problem because it does what?","correct":"Puts an unapproved drug pattern into a safety-sensitive job","w1":"Is identical to taking a medication as prescribed","w2":"Is encouraged if the shift is long","w3":"Cannot affect security duties","explain":"Authorized medical use and misuse are not the same. A safety or security duty depends on the member being fit for the task."},
{"ch":22,"sec":"22A","para":"22A","title":"Total Readiness","page":414,"diff":2,"question":"Chapter 22 links physical fitness with mental readiness because the mission needs what?","correct":"The whole person, not a single test score in isolation","w1":"Only a fast run","w2":"Only a high decoration count","w3":"Only time in grade","explain":"The chapter's sections move from the program, to the assessment, to food, substances, and mindset. One number does not carry the chapter."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24B","para":"24B","title":"The Flag","page":547,"diff":2,"demo":True,"question":"AFH 1's customs chapter treats the United States flag as something Airmen do what with?","correct":"Show specific respect, including how it is displayed, raised, and lowered","w1":"Use as a table cover when a cloth is missing","w2":"Dip to any visiting officer as a greeting","w3":"Alter with unit logos for morale","explain":"Section 24B is respect for the flag. Using it as a cover, dipping it improperly, or adding logos breaks that respect."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24C","para":"24C","title":"Salutes","page":554,"diff":2,"question":"A salute, in the courtesies AFH 1 teaches, is what?","correct":"A greeting and a sign of mutual respect, not a contest","w1":"A way to show who is annoyed","w2":"Required indoors while uncovered in ordinary office traffic","w3":"Owed only to people you like","explain":"Section 24C frames the salute as mutual respect exchanged under the rules of the custom. It is not a mood and it is not optional based on friendship."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24A","para":"24A","title":"Honored Traditions","page":543,"diff":2,"question":"Honored traditions in AFH 1 exist to do what?","correct":"Connect today's Airmen to the service's standards and history","w1":"Replace written orders","w2":"Give units permission to invent discourtesy","w3":"Apply only at basic training","explain":"Section 24A treats traditions as part of professional identity. They do not outrank law or excuse rudeness."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24D","para":"24D","title":"Reveille and Retreat","page":557,"diff":2,"question":"During retreat, AFH 1 expects Airmen outdoors in uniform to do what when the music sounds?","correct":"Face the flag or the music and render the proper courtesy","w1":"Keep walking because the duty day is over","w2":"Salute with the left hand","w3":"Sit down until the song ends","explain":"Section 24D covers ceremonies including reveille and retreat. The courtesy is part of the ceremony, not an interruption to ignore."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24E","para":"24E","title":"Drill","page":562,"diff":2,"question":"Drill and ceremonies matter in AFH 1 because they train what?","correct":"Precision, attention, and the habit of moving as one formation","w1":"Individual freestyle marching","w2":"How to avoid the chain of command","w3":"A substitute for technical training","explain":"Section 24E presents drill as disciplined movement. It does not replace upgrade training, and it is not freestyle."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24F","para":"24F","title":"Heritage","page":564,"diff":2,"question":"Preserving heritage, in the last section of AFH 1, asks Airmen to do what?","correct":"Learn the service's story and pass it on accurately","w1":"Memorize myths because they inspire more than facts","w2":"Ignore any history that is uncomfortable","w3":"Treat heritage as an officer-only subject","explain":"Section 24F closes the book on heritage. Accuracy is the respect owed to the people in that story."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24C","para":"24C","title":"Courtesy to People","page":554,"diff":2,"question":"Respect for individuals in the customs chapter means courtesy is owed to whom?","correct":"People, including juniors, peers, seniors, and those outside the service","w1":"Only officers","w2":"Only members of the same career field","w3":"Only people who salute first","explain":"Section 24C is respect for individuals, not a one-direction ritual. Courtesy still has rules, and those rules run both ways."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24B","para":"24B","title":"Half-Staff","page":547,"diff":3,"question":"Displaying the flag at half-staff is a custom used to signify what?","correct":"Mourning, when the nation or the service directs it","w1":"A routine Friday","w2":"A unit picnic","w3":"The start of a PT test","explain":"Half-staff is a mourning custom directed by proper authority. It is not a local decoration for an ordinary day."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24A","para":"24A","title":"Airman's Creed in Custom","page":543,"diff":2,"question":"The Airman's Creed sits in both professionalism and heritage discussions because it does what?","correct":"States a shared warrior identity Airmen are expected to know","w1":"Replaces the oath of enlistment","w2":"Is optional trivia from 1947","w3":"Is a classified document","explain":"Paragraph 1.5 gives the creed's origin, and Chapter 24's heritage sections expect Airmen to carry that identity forward. It does not replace the oath."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24D","para":"24D","title":"Ceremonies","page":557,"diff":2,"question":"A ceremony in AFH 1 is supposed to be run so that what happens?","correct":"The formation looks disciplined and the honor is unmistakable","w1":"Participants improvise the sequence","w2":"The audience cannot tell what is being honored","w3":"Rehearsal is considered disrespectful","explain":"Section 24D treats ceremonies as deliberate events. Rehearsal is how the honor stays clear."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24E","para":"24E","title":"Formation","page":562,"diff":2,"question":"In a formation, AFH 1's drill section expects the individual to do what?","correct":"Hold the position and move on command, not on a personal timeline","w1":"March at whatever speed feels natural","w2":"Talk through the command","w3":"Leave when bored","explain":"Drill only works if the formation shares one command. Personal timing is how intervals collapse."},
{"ch":24,"sec":"24C","para":"24C","title":"Titles","page":554,"diff":2,"question":"Using the correct title and courtesy in AFH 1 is a way of showing what?","correct":"Respect for the person and for the rank they hold","w1":"That you have memorized the pay chart for no reason","w2":"Fear","w3":"That juniors do not deserve a title","explain":"Section 24C connects titles and courtesies to respect. Juniors are still addressed properly. The habit is not fear."},
]
